<?xml version="1.0"?>
<CP_Scriptfile date="Jul 8, 2022">
<CPscript title="Maximal Packings of Infinite Complexes" >
<description>If a simply connected complex K does not triangulate a sphere or a closed topological disc, then it must be infinite. It is known that K has a maximal packing P nonetheless, but the geometry is determined by the combinatorics: K is hyperbolic if P lives in the unit disc, parabolic if in the euclidean plane. Here we try to understand the dichotomy.</description>
<Section title="Illustrating the Dichotomy">
<text> The constant 6-degree (hex) and constant 7-degree (hept) complexes illustrate that the combinatorics of K determine its geometry. (See "Type567.xmd" also.) </text>
<cmd name="*" mnemonic="*" iconname="centrejust.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;seed 6;max_pack;Disp -w -c -cfc5 1;act 1;seed 7;max_pack;disp -w;Disp -w -c -cfc5 1 </cmd>
<text> We take subcomplexes of K with increasing numbers of generations and compute their maximal packings in the unit disc. In p0 we start with a hexagonal flower, and in p1 with a heptagonal flower. Press '6' in p0 or '7' in p1 and an additional generation will be added to the pattern. Try these a few times, watch the action at the blue center circle, and see if you notice a difference in behavior. (Careful with '7', the number of circles grows rapidly and CirclePack can easily be swamped.)

An infinite packing would necessarily involve some limit process. In p0, this requires a rescalling, whereas in p1 the limit can be taken in place. The infinite hex packing is euclidean while the infinite hept packing is hyperbolic. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="A Sample Triangulation">
<text> What happens with less predictable combinatorics? Given an infinite simply connected complex K without boundary, there is a maximal packing P which is either euclidean or hyperbolic. Determining which is called the "type problem". 

The practical approach is illustrated here with a random triangulation K. The next two commands read K into p0 and its maximal packing P into p1. Click on a few vertices on the left and right to convince yourself that the packing P and the complex K correspond. </text>
<cmd iconname="kivio.png">act 0;Cleanse;Map 0 1;infile_read random.p;set_rad .025 a;color -c 195 1 2;Disp -w -f -cf 1 2; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="debugger.png">act 1;infile_read max.p;color -c 195 1 2;Disp -w -c -cf 1 2; </cmd>
<text> This is, of course, fake. If you zoom out, you will see that K extends some way beyond the initial screen, but then stops. Likewise for P in p1. And I will confess that P was computed by simply scaling up the usual max packing for this finite K. However, your job is to pretend that these are infinite objects, OK? 

We proceed by the method of exhaustion: that is, we work with a sequence of nested finite simply connected complexes K1, K2, ..., which are growing finite portions of K and "exhaust" K in the sense that any vertex of K is eventually in Kj for all large j. 

Each finite complex Kj has a maximal packing in the unit disc, and in each we put the alpha vertex 2240 at the origin (and vertex 2512 on the positive y-axis). The following commands illustrate the first few complexes and their packings in the disc with the alpha vertex highlighted. </text>
<cmd iconname="kuickshow.png">act 2;Map -x;cleanse;g:=28;[c];disp -w;Disp -c -cf 1 2; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="debugger.png">g:=27;[c];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="delete.png">g:=26;[c];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="network.png">g:=24;[c];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="run.png">g:=21;[c];disp -wr </cmd>
<cmd iconname="metacontact_offline.png">g:=17;[c];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="bookmark.png">g:=12;[c];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="idea.png">g:=6;[c];disp -wr; </cmd>
<cmd iconname="button_ok.png">copy -p0 2;max_pack;disp -w -c -cf 1 2 </cmd>
<text> Observe that as the number of generations increases, the alpha circle at the origin gets smaller. This is reminiscent of the hex case above and suggests that the limit packing would be obtained by rescaling. So the maximal packing would be euclidean, as is the infinite hex.

This shows how the dichotomy between types is resolved. But of course the limit process must be handled by some theory, since computation has its limits. The intuition gained in experiments led to the formulation of this type problem and to many situations where it can be settle. Perhaps the most recent result by Ed Crane and Mark Walters is pertinent here: 

Theorem: If K is a random Delaunay triangulation of the plane, then almost certainly K is parabolic.

This joins a number of other results, most of which have to do with the degrees of vertices in K or the rate of growth of generations of K. It remains an active area in circle packing research. </text>
</Section>
<Section title="Tools">
<cmd inline="no" name="6" mnemonic="6" tooltip="add 1 generation of degree 6" iconname="dnd_multi.png">add_gen -p0 1 6;max_pack -p0;disp -p0 -w -c -cfc5 1; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="7" mnemonic="7" tooltip="add 1 generation of degree 7" iconname="psi.png">add_gen -p1 1 7;max_pack -p1;disp -p1 -w -c -cfc5 1; </cmd>
<cmd inline="no" name="c" mnemonic="c" iconname="delete.png">act 2;copy -p0 2;gen_mark b;cookie -n {c:m.lt. _g};alpha 1;gamma 2;max_pack; </cmd>
</Section>
  </CPscript>
